<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahdist State</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1898">1898</a> (in Darfur)</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mahdist interregnum, Mahdist</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mahdist State was the state founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad">Muhammad Ahmad</a>, who proclaimed himself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> in 1881, and continued under his successor the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallahi-ibn-muhammad">Khalifa Abdallahi</a>. Following al-Zubayr's defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> sultan in 1874, Darfur was absorbed by the Mahdists, extinguishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keira-dynasty">Keira</a> authority. The site refers to the period from 1874 to 1898 in Darfur as the Mahdist interregnum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahdist-state.jpg" alt="Mahdist State" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Mahdi_movement_in_Sudan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> from the early 19th century, a period known locally as the Turkiyya. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> opened in 1869 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> sought a greater role in Egyptian affairs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Khedive Ismail</a> appointed the Briton Charles George Gordon governor-general of Sudan, whose opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> bankrupted many merchants. After Gordon resigned, discontent grew and tax collectors raised taxes arbitrarily. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad">Muhammad Ahmad</a>, the son of a Danagla boatbuilder and a sheikh of the Sammaniyah Sufi order, called for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> that found great appeal among the poorest communities along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>To avoid arrest the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> and his followers, the Ansar, marched to Kurdufan and won recruits, especially among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baggara-arabs">Baggara</a>. Early in 1882 the Ansar overwhelmed a 7,000-man Egyptian force near Al Ubayyid; the Mahdi then starved the town into submission after four months, and the Ansar later defeated an 8,000-man relief force at Sheikan. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a>, Baggara rebels besieged the governor-general Rudolf Carl von Slatin, who was captured in 1883. Gordon, sent back to evacuate the garrisons, chose to hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>. The Ansar stormed it in January 1885, killed him and destroyed the town, building a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> died, probably of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a>, on 22 June 1885. His deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallahi-ibn-muhammad">Abdallahi ibn Muhammad</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">Khalifa</a>, gained unchallenged supremacy in 1891 with the help of the Baqqara Arabs, purged the Mahdi's family and early disciples and met any hint of tribal disloyalty with reprisals, among them the massacre of the Juhaina. A 60,000-man army invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1887, but an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was defeated at Tushkah in 1889, and after an 1888 drought the country fell into famine. On 2 September 1898 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Herbert Kitchener</a>'s Anglo-Egyptian force destroyed the Khalifa's 52,000-man army outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a>, where about 11,000 Mahdists died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">Khalifa</a> died fighting at Umm Diwaykarat in November 1899, and Osman Digna resisted until January 1900. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a>, where the Mahdist era became known as the umkowakia, the period of chaos and anarchy, rule had already been collapsing amid rebellions led by Abu Jimeiza of the Tama, and the restored Sultanate of Darfur destroyed the last holdout, at Kabkabiya, in 1909.</p><p>Famine, disease, persecution and war had roughly halved the population: about 8 million people lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> before the revolt, and an Egyptian census afterwards recorded barely 2.5 million. The Mahdiyah has become known as the first genuine Sudanese nationalist government. It imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a>, added a declaration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> to the creed and severely persecuted Christians, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahdist State” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
